<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jamaa el Djedid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1660</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as New Mosque (Algiers), Djamaa el Djedid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jamaa el Djedid, or New Mosque, is a mosque on the waterfront of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, built in 1660 for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> rite followed by the Ottoman garrison and Turkish community of the Regency. Its large central dome and cross-shaped plan show Ottoman influence, combined with a square <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghrebi</a> minaret. It stands on the Place des Martyrs and remains one of the city's principal mosques.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jamaa-el-djedid.jpg" alt="Jamaa el Djedid" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ludovic Courtès · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alger-Place-des-Martyrs-Casbah_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
